A||N||10||87FieroGT||A Suspenseful question?||||3
Z||000000||87FieroGT||09-18-1999||08:28 PM||||I need to know what the best and most cost efficient suspension upgrade would be for my 87 GT? What are coil overs? And finally what is the best size tires to put on my Fiero.||||reg||3
Z||000001||David||09-18-1999||09:03 PM||||This topic will catch a lot of different opinions.  I won't try to answer the first part, but coil-overs are a shock-spring combination, using what would be considered a standard type of a shock with a spring seat on the lower part of the shock and another seat on the top part.  The spring then goes between the two seats.  Get yourself a Summit or Jegs catalog to see a picture.  As to what size tires to use, I'm still sruck with my 195-14's, but most of what I read seems to point to 215,225 for the rear and 205,215 for the front.  Some people at V8 Archie seem to think that Yokohama is the brand to have.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||09-19-1999||12:34 AM||||flat out.......... There is no best tire. Every single person will have a difernat answer to that. Mine is using some NTB/Sears Cooper made tires that are just fine for how I drive. ((which is pretty freaking hard.))<P>You will hear about "coil overs" for the rear. Fact is that all MacFerson(sp) Strut systems ARE "coil over." There is no other way to build them.<P>On the other hand installing Coil Over systems on the front of Fiero requires Major work. Coil Over setups require rework/replacement for the entire lower front control arm.||||reg||8
Z||000003||88 Fiero||09-19-1999||12:57 PM||||If you want to install coils over... is only when your going wider in the rear...like 9 inch...because it would not clear with the stock struts. Everybody is right as far as how they feel what size of wheels they want to put on their cars..Other people feels 15 inch is the right size and others want more...on Fiero's you can go as big as 18 inch all around.. I did a research on that..but I found out the 17 inch is more favorable to the car than 18 because your going to track more in 18 inch...I just sold my 17 inch OZ to another Fiero buddy and again I am getting another 17 inch and will go 7 1/2 in the front and 9 inch in the rear. Also check your offsets.. The '84 to '87 Fiero's have the same offsets.. 35 millimeters and the '88 are totally different. I have to shave my OZ's for another 5 millimeters for them not to stick out of the fenders.....||||reg||6
Z||000004||batboy||09-19-1999||01:14 PM||||The first thing you need to do is go back and read some of the old discussions here on the Forum. There is a ton of info in here. LowCG also has some tricks he might share with you. Good tires, shocks, and struts...along with replacing all worn out steering and suspension parts will do a world of good in improving performance handling. I'd forget the coil-overs unless you have lots of money and plan on racing.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Carrolles||09-20-1999||12:26 PM||||Probably the most cost effective suspension upgrade would be a rear sway bar.||||reg||1
Z||000006||RICK||09-20-1999||01:12 PM||||What is the widest tire/wheel combination you can put on the rear of the Fiero without it touching or rubing anything? I would like to use a 15" or 16" tall wheel<BR>thanks||||reg||1
Z||000007||Formula||09-20-1999||01:18 PM||||I have 15" wheels with 7" in the front and 7.5" in the rear. Tires are 215s all around. I think they are firestones. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Dale||09-22-1999||02:35 AM||||Rick,<BR>I would suggest going 15" or 17" forget the 16".  Thier are a lot more tire selections in 15' and 17".||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||09-22-1999||07:57 AM||||I've heard of people running 235-60-15 tires on the rear of Fieros before. You have to watch the off-set on the wheels your using.||||reg||1
Z||000010||VISCERAL||09-22-1999||08:52 AM||||Hey 88 FIERO, Do you have rear coilovers? I do. I have a lot mor clearance than stock. I could even shave a bit off my strut perch if I need more room. What is your offset? I read this on West Coast Fieros FAQ. Does this sound right?<P>Q. What is the largest wheel and tire that I can put on my Fiero?<BR>                   A. We have experienced the maximum wheel and tire on the front of a Fiero as 17 x 8 with a 45mm offset.  This car has 235x40x17 tires.  This wheel just touches the lower A-arm in maximum turning and the tire just misses the top of inner fender well at maximum up-wheel travel.  This combination keeps the tire inside the front wheel well.<BR>                       <BR>On the rear,  we have experienced 17 x 9 with a 45mm offset. This car has 255x40x17 tires.  This diameter of the wheel is needed to clear the upright on the rear suspension. 16 inch wheels will notclear at 9 inches in width.  This combination will keep the tire inside the rear wheel well.<P>I would probably go with 225/45/17 in front and then 255/40/17 in the rear||||reg||10
